## Acute myeloid leukaemia in adults: Overview

- **Epidemiology** - most common type of acute leukaemia in adults (80%):
  - <u>Incidence</u> - relatively rare disease at 4 cases/100000/y (1% of adult malignancies)
  - <u>Demographic</u>:
    - Age - incidence increases w/ age (median age of Dx 68y)
    - Sex - modest male preponderance
- **Pre-malignant conditions** - AML may be preceded by clonal haematopoeisis manifested as:
  - Myelodysplastic syndromes
  - Myeloproliferative neoplasms
  - Paroxysmal nocturnal haemoglobinuria
  - Aplastic anaemia
  - Clonal haematopoesis of indeterminate prognosis (CHIP)
  - Clonal cytopenia of unknown significance (CCUS)
- **Cancer predisposing syndromes a/w AML:**
  - Trisomy 21
  - Fanconi anaemia
  - Li-Fraumeni syndrome
  - Bloom syndrome
- **Pathophysiology of AML:**
  - **Characteristic mutations in AML** - specific chromosomal abnormalities are identified in \> 50% of cases of AML which serve as driver mutations (while point mutations not ameable to detection by karyotyping is seen in nearly all cases)
  - **Transformation of myeloid precursors to leukaemic stem cells** - mutations results in 1) <u>increased proliferation</u>, 2) <u>arrested maturation</u>, and 3) <u>evasion of apoptosis</u>, which result in self-renewal, and generation of their progeny, i.e. the bulk population of leukaemic cells
- **Principles of clinical presentation of AML:**
  - **Clinical features relating to marrow failure:**
    - <u>Asymptomatic</u> - presents w/ abnormal CBC with differentials, either exhibiting as pancytopenia or bicytopenia with leukocytosis
    - <u>Features of anaemia</u> - acute onset of anaemia manifesting as weakness or dyspnoea a/w marked pallor on P/E
    - <u>Features of thrombocytopenia</u> - a/w excess mucocutaneous bleeding on trauma or bruising
    - <u>Features of neutropenia</u> - presenting as fever (neutropenic fever) and underlying infection
  - **Clinical features relating to complications of AML:**
    - <u>Leukostasis</u> - respiratory +/- neurological distress in patients w/ AML and WBC \> 100 x 10<sup>9</sup>/L
    - <u>Tumour lysis syndrome</u> - excessive cellular turnover results in metabolic derrangements
    - <u>Bleeding</u> - caused by severe thrombocytopenia or DIC, which may be related to the leukaemia itself (i.e. APML) or underlying sepsis
    - <u>CNS involvement</u> - e.g. cerebral haemorrhage
- **Acute promyelocytic leukaemia** (APML) - a distinct subtype of AML that should be suspected in younger patients (40-50y) especially if presenting w/ spontaneous bleeding (or bleeding out-of-proportion to known trauma)
- **Pre-treatment evaluation** - identification of pathological features and comorbidities that might alter Mx and affect prognosis:
  - **Routine bloods** - CBC, LRFT, CaPO4, clotting profile, viral serologies
  - **Bone marrow examination** - BM aspiration and trephine Bx for 1) morphology, 2) karyotype, 3) flow cytometry, and 4) molecular genetics
  - **Additional pre-treatment evaluation:**
    - <u>CXR</u>
    - <u>ECG and echocardiogram</u>
    - <u>G6PD deficiency</u>
    - <u>HLA typing</u> - for patients who are potential candidates for HSC transplantation
- **Medical fitness** - guides Tx intent:
  - <u>Age</u> - not absolute C/I for intensive therapy, but cautions in those \>= 70y because of associated comorbidities (most trials of AML exclude patients \> 60y)
  - <u>Performance status</u> - assessment of daily functioning, e.g. by ECOG PS
  - <u>Comorbidities</u> - account for comorbidities (e.g. w/ Charlson's Comorbidity Index, or HST-CI index)
- **Dx** - documentation of \>= 10% myeloblasts in bone marow or blood that exhibit characteristic pathological features, or detection of myeloid sarcoma
- **Dx criteria** - different diagnostic criteria over time for Dx and classification of suspected AML which differ slightly:
  - International Consensus Classification
  - World Health Organization 5th edition criteria (most commonly used currently)
  - French-American-British scheme (historical interest)
- **DDx of AML:**
  - <u>Other haematological malignancies</u> - esp. MDS or other acute leukaemia as clinical presentation and cellular morphology of MDS may resemble those AML but can be distinguished by karyotyping, immunophenotyping and molecular features (consider high-grade lymphomas if presenting w/ tumour lysis syndrome)
  - <u>Other causes of pancytopenia</u> - marrow or splenic pathologies
- **Prognosis:**
  - **Prognostic features** - clinical, cytogenetic/ molecular features determine likelihood of achieving CR and long-term survival:
    - <u>Clinical features</u> - age, PS, medical comorbidities, prior myelodysplastic syndrome, and exposure to radiation or cytotoxic agents
    - <u>Cytogenetic/ molecular features</u> - European LeukemiaNet further stratifies patients into favourable, intermediate or adverse risks based on the cytogenetic/ molecular features of myeloblasts
  - **European LeukemiaNet prognostic categories:** 
  ![](~/Documents/notes/img/european_leukemianet_prognostic_categories.png)
- **Approach to Mx:**
  - **Intent of Tx** - dependent on age and medical fitness of the patient:
    - <u>Fit, younger patient</u> - Tx of curative intent which entails 1) remission induction therapy, 2) response assessment, and 3) consolidation therapy (+/- maintenance therapy)
    - <u>Less fit, or older patients</u> - Tx of palliative intent w/ low-intensity therapy based on hypomethylating agents + venetoclax
- **Assessment of Tx response to induction therapy:**
  - **Nadir marrow assessment** - BM aspirate and Bx at 14-21d after beginning induction therapy to assess cellularity and percentage of blasts (must be interpreted when not hypocellular marrow), guiding further Mx:
    - <u>Blasts \<= 5%</u> - continue supportive care, and monitor and Tx post-Tx complications as they come, before formal response assessment as blood counts recover
    - <u>Blasts \> 5%</u> - second cycle induction therapy considered if tolerable in the presence of substantial persistance of blasts, and early consideration of allogenic HSCT if remission is obtained as categorised as 'primary refractory'
  - **Response assessment** - repeat assessment of blood and marrow as blood counts recover:
    - <u>Cytological complete response</u> - \< 5% of blasts and marrow blasts
    - <u>Cytogenetic complete response</u> - dissapearance of any cells w/ abnormal pretreatment karyotype
    - <u>Molecular response</u> - minimal measurable residual disease (MRD) on immunohistochemistry (flow cytometry) or molecular techniques
- **Relapse/ refractory AML** - r/r AML carries grave prognosis with allogenic HSCT being the only potential cure:
  - <u>Relapsed AML</u> - disease recurrence after achievement of complete remission
  - <u>Refractory AML</u> - failure of achieving CR w/ two cycles of remission induction therapy

## Acute myeloid leukaemia: Clinical manifestations, pathological features, and diagnosis

- **Clinical features of AML** - Sx related to 1) cytopenias, 2) extramedullary disease, or 3) compications a/w high volume disease:
  - **Features related to cytopenia:**
    - <u>Anaemia</u> - fatigue, weakness are common and often preceded the diagnosis of AML by months, ocassionaly may present w/ SOB if acute in onset
    - <u>Neutropenia</u> - manifests as fever w/ or w/o a clinical foci of infection (i.e. neutropenic fever)
    - <u>Bleeding</u> - primarily due to thrombocytopenia, but quantitative platelet defects and coagulopathies (esp. in APML) do contribute
  - **Features of extra-medullary disease** - manifestation of myeloid sarcoma, organ infiltration, infiltration into skin and gingiva, and CNS disruption:
    - <u>Myeloid sarcoma</u> - tumour infiltration, commonly in LN, spleen liver, skin and gingiva (acute monoblastic leukaemia), and rarely in the orbit (orbital pain), intestine (IO) or other solid organs
    - <u>Bone and joint pain</u> (\< 5%) - multiple processes contribute including gout, pseudogout, infections, or direct infiltration w/ leukaemic cells
    - <u>Skin</u> - most commonly pale due to anaemia w/ petechiae or echymosis but leukaemic involvement of skin may occur:
      - **Leukaemic cutis** (myeloid sarcoma) - nodular, and violaceous/ gray-blue lesions often found in patients w/ prominent monocytic or myelomonocytic component
      - **Leukocytoclastic vasculitis** - very rare cause of small vessel vasculitis manifesting as palpable purpura
      - **Acute neutrophilic dermatosis** (Sweet syndrome) - widespread erythematous tender nodules and plaques on skin

      
      ![](~/Documents/notes/img/leukemia_cutis.png)
    - <u>Oropharynx</u> - gingival hypertrophy a/w monocytic subtypes, or as site of infections (e.g. oral candidiasis, herpetic lesions)
    - <u>Abdominal organomegaly</u> - e.g. hepatosplenomegaly occurs only occurs in \< 10% of cases
    - <u>Lymphadenopathy</u> - palpable adenopathy is uncommon and should raise suspicion of another idangosis
  - **Manifestation as medical emergencies:**
    - <u>Hyperleukocytosis/ leukostasis</u> - sudden onset of respiratory or CNS distress and other end-organ leukostatic effects
    - <u>Neurological complications</u> - various neurological complications include 1) CNS haemorrhage, 2) myeloid sarcoma, and 3) leukaemic meningitis
    - <u>Tumour lysis syndrome</u> - metabolic and electrolyte abnormalities due to high turnover of leukaemic cells
- **Initial Ix** - on suspected leukaemia:
  - **Routine bloods** - CBC, PBS, LRFT, clotting profile and fibrinogen, LDH, uric acid, CaPO4:
    - <u>CBC</u> - picture of 1) pancytopenia, or 2) leukocytosis (leukemic cells)
    - <u>PBS</u> - identification and quantification of circulating blasts (+/- cytomorphological, genetic, cytogenetic and molecular analysis)
    - <u>LFT</u> - baseline liver function
    - <u>RFT</u> - electrolyte disturbances, esp. HyperK (N.B. suprious hyperK is possible and repeat K if in absence of Sx or ECG changes)
    - <u>Clotting profiles, fibrinogen</u> - detection of DIC
    - <u>LDH, uric acid, PO4</u> - detection of cellular turnover
  - **Imaging** - CXR, CT T+A+P:
    - <u>CXR</u> - esp. if presenting w/ chest Sx suggesting of chest infections
    - <u>CT T+A+P</u> - seldom obtained but can be performed if suspecting myeloid sarcoma
  - **Bone marrow examination** - BM aspirate and trephine Bx for pathological examination which is used for:
    - Cytomorphological diagnosis
    - Immunophenotyping
    - Cytogenetic analysis
    - Molecular analysis
  - **Additional Ix** - e.g. w/ neurological involvement:
    - <u>CT brain</u> - if presenting w/ neurological Sx
    - <u>LP</u> - if presenting w/ neurological Sx and meningism (N.B. bleeding tendancies must be corrected)
- **Peripheral blood smear** (PBS) - identification of circulating blasts by an experienced haematopathologist:
  - **Blood counts:**
    - <u>WBC</u> - variable, some w/ leukopenia while some have leukocytosis
    - <u>RBC</u> - normocytic, normochromic anaemia
    - <u>PLT</u> - typically thrombocytopenia (75%)
  - **Morphology** - identification of circulating blasts by automated haematology analyzers which identify:
    - Immature cells w/ large nuclei, prominent nucleoli and variable amount of pale blue cytoplasm w/ faint granulations
    - Auer rods (pink-rad granular structures) are pathognomic for AML
    - Prominent cytoplasmic granules are seen in APML
  - **Immunophenotyping:**
    - <u>Myeloperoxidase</u> (MPO) - cytochemical reaction to identify myeloid lineage (superceded by flow cytometry)
    - <u>Flow cytometry</u> - for automated lineage assignment
- **Bone-marrow examination** - aspirate and trephine biopsy for Dx and subtyping of AML:
  - **Cytomorphology** - quantification of blasts (as above) and identify other histological features of AML
  - **Cytogenetics** - identified of chromosomal anomalies by 1) karyotyping or 2) FISH, to identify <u>defining genetic characteristics</u>
  - **Immunophenotyping** - AML blasts must demonstrate immunophenotypic features of myeloid, monocytic, erythroid, or megakaryocytic lineages:
    - <u>Expression of myeloid markers</u> - most cases express CD34, HLA-DR, CD117, MPO, CD13, and CD33, although the specific pattern differs among AML subtypes
    - <u>Co-expression of lymphoid markers</u> - seen in 20% of AML (e.g. CD7, CD19, CD79a, PAX5, CD2 and CD4), where expression may be associated w/ specific AML subtypes:
      - CD19 w/ RUNX1::RUNX1T1
      - CD2 with APML
  - **Molecular analysis** - by NGS to detect mutations which may guide choice of induction therapy for AML (e.g. FLT3, IDH1/2)
- **Approach to AML:**
  - <u>Dx of AML</u> - a pathological diagnosis based on blast percentage in PBS and BM examination, where blasts are defined morphologically (e.g. Aurer rods), or immunophenotypically (e.g. expression of myeloid markers on flow cytometry)
  - <u>AML subtyping and prognoostication</u> - further subtyping based on 1) cytogenetics, 2) immunophenotyping, and 3) molecular analysis, which may allow risk stratification of AML (and occassionally guide induction Tx)
  - <u>Determing Tx intent</u> - shared decision making in decision to pursue intensive therapy of curative intent, or palliative therapy based on 1) clinical factors, and 2) cytogenetic and molecular factors
  - <u>Supportive care</u> - symptomatic Mx for all patients irrespective of Tx intent (e.g. transfusions, Tx of infections etc.)
  - <u>Pre-Tx evaluation</u> - evaluation of AML-related complications and emergencies, and comorbidities that affect tolerance for intensive remission induction therapy
- **WHO5 classification of AML subtypes:**
  - **Classification by genetic abnormalities or degree of differentiation:**
    - <u>Defining genetic abnormalities</u> - AML if presence of AML-defining genetic abnormalities even \< 20% blasts (requires \>= 10% blasts)
    - <u>Myeloid sarcoma</u> - extramedullary collection of myeloid blasts that satisfy morphologic, immunophenotypic or defining cytogenetic features of AML
    - <u>Classification by level of differentiation</u> - if in the absence of defining genetic abnormality which requires \> 20% blasts to define as AML
  - **Special categories:**
    - <u>Therapy-related myeloid neoplasms</u> - role of exposure to prior cytotoxic and radiation therapy
    - <u>AML with germline predispositions</u> - germline predispositions including:
      - Myeloid malignancies w/ germline pathogenic variants
      - Various bone marrow failure syndrome
    - <u>Myeloid proliferations related to Down syndrome</u> - in association w/ triosomy 21

## Acute myeloid leukemia: Induction therapy in fit, younger adults

- **Approach to pre-treatment evaluation** - in a young, fit adult, evaluation for <u>AML-related complications</u> and <u>comorbidities that can affect tolerance for intensive remission induction therapy</u>:
  - **Clinical assessmet** - detailed Hx and P/E to evaluate comorbid illnesses and other conditions that might affect Mx:
    - **Comorbid medical diseases** - heart, liver, and kidney disease:
      - <u>Heart disease</u> - special attention to cardiac function as routine use of anthracycline (cardiotoxic), and often excessive fluid administration (fluid overload)
      - <u>Liver disease</u> - affects dosing and schedule of anthracycline administration
      - <u>Kidney disease</u> - affects dosing and scheduling and influences Mx of tumour lysis syndrome
      - <u>Infections</u> - HBV, HCV, HSV, CMV, HIV and TB may reactivate in setting of immunosuppression
    - **Prior haematological or malignant disorders** - Mx of therapy-related AML differs
    - **Drug allergy** - esp. ABx as guides selection of ABx
    - **Prior drug transfusions** - alloimmunisation resulting in transfusion reactions
  - **Pre-Tx Ix** - see details below
  - **Assessment of medical fitness** - fitness for intensive remission induction therapy determined by performance status and comorbidities:
    - <u>Performance status</u> - assessed by ECOG PS or Karnofsky PS
    - <u>Comorbidities</u> - various instruments including Charlson's Comorbidity Index (CCI), HCT-CI, and Ferrara criteria (AML-specific)
- **Pre-treatment Ix:**
  - **Routine bloods** - CBC and PBS, LRFT, clotting profile, CaPO4, LDH, uric acid, RBG:
    - <u>CBC and PBS</u> - for Dx and detection of complications (e.g. hyperleukocytosis, severe thrombocytopenia)
    - <u>LFT</u> - detection of liver disease which require dose adjustment
    - <u>RFT</u> - detection of hyperK, and baseline renal function
    - <u>Clotting profile</u> - detection of DIC (+/- fibrinogen and D-dimer)
    - <u>LDH, uric acid</u> - to assess leukaemia cell burden and baseline cell turnover
    - <u>CaPO4</u> - to detect hyperphosphataemia
  - **Additional Ix:**
    - <u>Viral serologies</u> - HBV, HCV, HIV
    - <u>G6PD assay</u> - detect G6PD deficiency as use of cotrimoxazole may be needed in profound neutropenia after induction therapy
    - <u>CXR</u> - detection of chest infections and pre-existing lung diseases
    - <u>Cardiac evaluation</u> (ECG, echocardiogram):
      - ECG - arrhythmias and baseline QTc prolongation noted
      - Echocardiogram - cardiac functions determined by LVEF on echo
    - <u>Neurological Ix</u> - not routine if asymptomatic (CT brain, LP)
    - <u>Dental evaluation</u> - to address possible infectious foci
    - <u>HLA typing</u> - for patients who may be candidates for HSCT
- **Induction therapy:**
  - **Standard regimen** - '7+3 regimen' as 1L based on acceptable toxicity and long-track record of efficacy for achieving CR:
    - 7 day infusion of cytarabine
    - Anthracycline (daunorubicin or idarubicin) on days 1-3
  - **Dosing of standard regimen:**
    - <u>Cytarabine</u> - 100-200 mg/m2/d continuous intravenous infusion for D1-7
    - <u>Anthracycline</u>:
      - Daunorubicin - 60-90 mg/m2/d IV on D1-3
      - Idarubicin - 12 mg/m2 IV on D1-3
  - **Adverse effects of standard regimen:**
    - <u>General adverse effects of cytotoxic therapy</u>:
      - GI upset - N/V, diarrhoea
      - Mucositis and stomatitis
      - Alopecia
      - Profound neutropenia (lasting 3-5 weeks)
    - <u>Adverse effects of cytarabine:</u>
      - Flu-like syndrome (fever)
      - Rash
    - <u>Adverse effects of daunorubicin:</u>
      - Infusion reaction
      - Cardiac arrhythmias
  - **Adjunctive therapies for specific AMLs:**
    - <u>FLT3 mutated AML</u> - addition of FLT-3 inhibitor (midostarurin)
    - <u>CD33 positive, CBF-rearranged AML</u> \[t(8;21), inv(16), t(16;16)\] - addition of gemtuzumab ozygamicin (an ADC liked to anti-CD33 mAb)
